WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.740
In this video, you resize a virtual machine on SUSE Virtualization through Git: the change is reviewed and merged, and Rancher Fleet applies it with CPU and memory hot-plug.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:20.350
The virtual machine demo-web-01 runs with one vCPU and two gigabytes of memory. Rancher Fleet created it from a manifest in Git.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:27.730
In the Git repository, the request form turned a resize request into this pull request, opened by the GitHub Actions bot.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:32.160
Open the pull request to see what was asked for, and by whom.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.030
The request records who asked, the change ticket with its reason, and that it applies when the pull request merges.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:44.020
The code owners file routes the review to the team that owns the virtual machines, here the repository owner.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:51.960
The validate check tested the manifest against the policy limits and posted a plan: two vCPUs and four gigabytes after the merge.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.760
Open Files changed to see the exact edit to the manifest.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:04.100
Only two lines change: CPU sockets go from one to two, and guest memory from two to four gigabytes.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:11.110
Return to the conversation. In the meantime, the code owner reviewed the change from their own account.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:17.790
The approval, with its comment, is now part of the pull request's history, next to the plan and the passing check.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:24.090
The code owner then merged it into the branch that Fleet watches. That commit is now the desired state.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:32.470
Back on the virtual machine, the flavor now reads two vCPUs and four gigabytes. The cluster adds them with a live migration.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:37.520
Open the Migration tab to see how the new size reached the running virtual machine.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:47.680
An automated workload update live-migrated the virtual machine to another node with the extra CPU and memory. It never restarted.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:56.993
The resize went from a reviewed pull request to the running virtual machine without a reboot. Git keeps the record of who asked, who approved, and what changed.
